---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
const bodies = [
  {name:"Asteroids",where:"Mostly the main asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter, but also near-Earth space and other orbital groups.",detail:"Rocky or metallic remnants and fragments. Their sizes range from tiny stones to bodies hundreds of kilometres across. They are not all located in one belt."},
  {name:"Comets",where:"Often originate in distant reservoirs; their orbits can bring them into the inner Solar System.",detail:"Icy bodies that can develop a coma and tails when sunlight warms their surfaces. The tails are shaped by sunlight and the solar wind, and generally point away from the Sun."},
  {name:"Meteoroids",where:"Small natural objects travelling through interplanetary space.",detail:"When a meteoroid enters an atmosphere and produces a visible streak of light, the phenomenon is called a meteor. A surviving fragment that reaches the ground is a meteorite."},
  {name:"Trojan asteroids",where:"Near stable regions associated with a planet's orbit, especially Jupiter's leading and trailing Lagrange points.",detail:"These objects share a planet's orbit while occupying gravitationally stable regions roughly 60 degrees ahead of or behind the planet."},
  {name:"Kuiper Belt",where:"Beyond Neptune, in the distant outer Solar System.",detail:"A broad population of icy bodies, including Pluto, and the source region for many short-period comets. It contains a range of dynamical populations."},
  {name:"Scattered disc",where:"A distant region beyond Neptune, linked to gravitational scattering by the giant planets.",detail:"Objects often have eccentric and inclined orbits, with dynamical histories shaped by Neptune and other gravitational interactions."},
  {name:"Detached objects",where:"At large distances beyond Neptune.",detail:"Their orbits are less strongly coupled to Neptune's present-day scattering than typical scattered-disc objects. Sedna is a famous example of a detached extreme object."},
  {name:"Oort Cloud",where:"A hypothesised, roughly spherical reservoir at enormous distances from the Sun.",detail:"It has not been directly observed as a cloud. Its existence is inferred from the orbital distribution of long-period comets and models of Solar System evolution."}
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Asteroids, comets and the outer frontier" description="Explore asteroids, comets, meteoroids, Trojans, the Kuiper Belt, scattered disc, detached objects and the hypothesised Oort Cloud.">
  <div class="container breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/explore/">Explore</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Small bodies & frontier</span></div>
  <header class="page-intro container"><div class="eyebrow">Small Solar System bodies · Beyond Neptune</div><h1>The leftovers are<br />part of the story.</h1><p class="lede">Asteroids, comets and distant icy objects are not debris without purpose. They preserve clues to how the Solar System formed and how its planets migrated and evolved.</p></header>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="region-banner"><div class="region-sun"></div><div class="region-orbit"></div><div class="region-copy"><span class="eyebrow">A system of populations</span><h2>Not one belt.<br />Many orbital families.</h2><p>Small bodies occupy different orbital regions and dynamical populations. Their paths can be shaped by planets, resonances, collisions and the Sun's gravity.</p><span class="region-caption">Conceptual illustration · Not to scale</span></div></div>
    <div class="body-grid">{bodies.map((body,i)=><article class="card body-card"><span class="body-index">{String(i+1).padStart(2,"0")}</span><h3>{body.name}</h3><span class="body-where">{body.where}</span><p>{body.detail}</p></article>)}</div>
    <div class="prose body-prose">
      <h2>Three words that are easy to mix up</h2>
      <p><strong>Meteoroid:</strong> the small natural object while it is travelling through space. <strong>Meteor:</strong> the streak of light and associated atmospheric phenomenon created when it enters an atmosphere. <strong>Meteorite:</strong> a piece that survives the journey and reaches the ground.</p>
      <h2>What are comet tails?</h2>
      <p>As a comet approaches the Sun, volatile ices near its surface can turn directly into gas. Escaping gas carries dust away, creating a coma and often more than one tail. The ion tail is shaped strongly by the solar wind and magnetic field; the dust tail is influenced by sunlight pressure and the comet's orbital motion. A comet's tails generally point away from the Sun, not necessarily behind its direction of travel.</p>
      <h2>The Kuiper Belt is not the Oort Cloud</h2>
      <p>The Kuiper Belt is a broad, flattened population of icy objects beyond Neptune. The Oort Cloud is a hypothesised distant, roughly spherical reservoir proposed to explain the source of many long-period comets. They are different structures, at very different scales and with different observational status.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Scientific status matters.</strong><p>The Oort Cloud has not been directly imaged or observed as a bounded cloud. It is inferred from comet orbits and dynamical models. ORRERYA labels it as hypothesised, not as a confirmed photographically mapped region.</p></div>
      <h2>Where does the heliosphere fit?</h2>
      <p>The heliosphere is the vast bubble-like region shaped by the solar wind as it interacts with interstellar space. It is not a belt of asteroids or a gravitational wall. The Sun's gravity can bind distant objects well beyond the heliosphere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="source-panel"><div><span class="eyebrow">Further reading</span><h3>Explore NASA's small-body science</h3><p>Mission data and population descriptions can change as new objects are discovered.</p></div><ul class="source-list"><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/asteroids-comets-meteors/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Asteroids, Comets & Meteors ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/kuiper-belt/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Kuiper Belt ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/oort-cloud/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Oort Cloud ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/heliosphere/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Heliosphere ↗</a></li></ul></div>
  </section>
  <style>.region-banner{min-height:310px;position:relative;display:flex;align-items:center;padding:32px 42px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:9px;overflow:hidden;background:radial-gradient(ellipse at 85% 50%,rgba(42,75,117,.34),transparent 48%),#0a1220}.region-sun{position:absolute;width:85px;height:85px;left:72%;top:46%;border-radius:50%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);background:radial-gradient(circle at 30% 28%,#fff1b8,#e5a451 60%,#8c482e);box-shadow:0 0 40px #e8a85288}.region-orbit{position:absolute;width:460px;height:240px;left:72%;top:46%;border:1px solid rgba(196,213,239,.2);border-radius:50%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%) rotate(-24deg);box-shadow:0 0 0 45px rgba(196,213,239,.025),0 0 0 110px rgba(196,213,239,.025)}.region-copy{max-width:470px;position:relative;z-index:1}.region-copy h2{font-size:2.7rem;margin:10px 0}.region-copy p{max-width:390px;font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.7}.region-caption{display:block;color:var(--quiet);font-size:.68rem;margin-top:22px}.body-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:13px;margin-top:20px}.body-card{padding:19px;position:relative}.body-index{font-size:.68rem;color:var(--gold)}.body-card h3{margin:14px 0 8px;font:600 1.15rem "Space Grotesk",sans-serif}.body-where{display:block;color:var(--cyan);font-size:.7rem;line-height:1.5}.body-card p{font-size:.79rem;line-height:1.7;margin:12px 0 0}.body-prose{margin-top:55px}.source-panel{margin-top:40px}@media(max-width:900px){.body-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}.region-sun{left:86%}.region-orbit{left:86%}}@media(max-width:550px){.body-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.region-banner{padding:25px 20px;min-height:340px}.region-sun{left:100%;opacity:.55}.region-orbit{left:100%;opacity:.5}.region-copy h2{font-size:2.2rem}}</style>
</SiteLayout>
